---
// Help: signing in, the account page, roles, sessions and notifications (docs/ACCOUNTS.md,
// website/worker/account/, src/pages/account.astro; who the moderator's rules trust: oscr/moderation.py).
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { REVIEW_DAYS } from "../../lib/moderation";

const toc = [
  { id: "why", text: "Why sign in" },
  { id: "providers", text: "ORCID, GitHub or Google" },
  { id: "kept", text: "What signing in keeps" },
  { id: "link", text: "Linking several identities" },
  { id: "roles", text: "Roles" },
  { id: "page", text: "Your account page" },
  { id: "notifications", text: "Notifications" },
  { id: "sessions", text: "Sessions and signing out" },
  { id: "limits", text: "Current limits" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Signing in and your account"
  description={`Signing in to ${SITE_NAME} with ORCID, GitHub or Google, without an email address: what is kept, the roles, the account page and its notifications.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/accounts/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    You can read the site without an account. You sign in to contribute: to submit a paper, claim it, correct its links,
    validate its map, add the badge or ask for a removal. You sign in with an identity you already have.{" "}
    {SITE_NAME} does not ask for your email address.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why sign in</h2>
  <p>
    A contribution changes a public record. The registry therefore needs to know who asks, and in which role (an author
    of the paper, a maintainer of its code, or someone else), so that it can answer them. For this it uses the identity
    providers that researchers already use. The registry has no passwords of its own.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">ORCID, GitHub or Google</h2>
  <dl class="terms">
    <dt>ORCID</dt>
    <dd>The best choice for an author: when a paper's metadata lists your ORCID iD, you are recognized as its author at
      once. {SITE_NAME} asks ORCID for the <code>openid</code> scope only, your iD and your public name.</dd>
    <dt>GitHub</dt>
    <dd>The way to be recognized as a maintainer of a repository on GitHub. {SITE_NAME} asks GitHub for no scope, so it
      gets public information only. It reads your account's number, login and name. It does not read your email
      address, even a public one.</dd>
    <dt>Google</dt>
    <dd>For anyone else. {SITE_NAME} asks Google for the <code>openid</code> scope only. Google then gives an opaque
      identifier and no name.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    Each sign-in goes to the provider's own page and comes back to the page you were on. It is protected against forged
    or replayed returns (a short signed cookie holds the flow's secrets for ten minutes). While the platform is being
    built, sign-in with ORCID may use ORCID's sandbox, a test service, and then says so; anything validated through it
    is recorded as a test.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kept">What signing in keeps</h2>
  <p>
    The registry keeps the name the provider gives (ORCID's public name, GitHub's name or login; Google gives none), your
    public handles (ORCID iD, GitHub login), each provider's identifier for you, your roles, your requests and their
    answers, and for each session a short description of the browser ("Firefox on macOS").
  </p>
  <p>
    It does not keep an email address or a password. It uses the provider's access token during the sign-in only and
    then drops it. It does not keep your session's identifier itself: the database keeps only its SHA-256 (a
    fingerprint of the identifier from which the identifier cannot be read back).
  </p>
  <p>The <a href="/privacy/#accounts">privacy page</a> lists every column of every table.</p>

  <h2 id="link">Linking several identities</h2>
  <p>
    Signed in, choose another provider on your account page: its identity joins your account, so that, for instance, an
    author who signed in with ORCID can also prove maintainership on GitHub. One identity per provider; an identity
    already linked to another account is refused.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roles">Roles</h2>
  <dl class="terms">
    <dt>Member</dt>
    <dd>Everyone signed in: may submit a paper, claim authorship, and ask for a removal.</dd>
    <dt>Verified author</dt>
    <dd>Of a given paper: automatically, when your account's ORCID iD is among the paper's authors in its published
      metadata, checked at each sign-in, and again with "Check my papers again" on your account page. Otherwise, by a{" "}
      <a href="/help/claim/">claim</a>, verified by the registry's rules when Crossref's automatic update has put the
      paper in your ORCID record, or accepted by the operator. A verified author may correct the paper's links, validate
      its map, and have their removal requests applied at once.</dd>
    <dt>Maintainer</dt>
    <dd>Of a given repository: on GitHub, checked at once with your GitHub identity, you own it, are a public member of
      the organization that owns it, are among its contributors, or authored a commit in it. Otherwise, and elsewhere
      (GitLab, Codeberg…), your claim waits for the operator, {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most, then is closed with how to be
      checked again. Only a maintainer who owns the repository or is a public member of its organization (or one the
      operator made) may correct the links of the papers whose code it is, for that repository, and have a removal of its
      copies applied at once. A contributor or the author of a commit cannot do these two things
      (<a href="/policies/moderation/#claims">why</a>).</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="page">Your account page</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/account/">Your account page</a> shows who you are signed in as, your linked identities, your roles, the
    papers that list your ORCID iD, the form to claim a repository, and every request you made (submissions,
    corrections, validations, claims and removal requests) with its status and the answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notifications">Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    Notifications appear in the site only: we decided that {SITE_NAME} sends no email. Today, the answers to your
    requests appear on your account page and on the pages concerned (a removal request's page shows its decision and its
    words). Separate notifications about new activity on your papers will come later, also in the site only.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sessions">Sessions and signing out</h2>
  <p>
    A session lasts 30 days from your last visit (renewed at most once a day), in a cookie your browser sends to this
    site only and that page scripts cannot read. A second cookie, which holds no secret, tells the pages that you are
    signed in. Without it, the pages of a signed-out reader send no request to the site's server. "Sign out" on your
    account page ends the session and deletes it. The <a href="/privacy/#cookies">privacy page</a> lists the cookies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Current limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You cannot yet unlink an identity from the site itself, or sign out of every browser at once. You can delete
      your account: ask for it on <a href="/data-rights/">your data and your rights</a>, and it is deleted within about
      ten minutes, with its sessions, identities, roles, claims and requests.</li>
    <li>Maintainer checks outside GitHub are not automatic: such a claim waits for the operator,{" "}
      {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most, then is closed. Archives (Zenodo, OSF) have no owner in their address, so a
      maintainer cannot claim them.</li>
  </ul>
</Info>
